WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.380
Pack sprouting peas into a thermos.

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.920
A day or two later, the thermometer can climb above room temperature.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.400
Boiled peas in another thermos stay close to the room.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.300
Peas have no muscles.

5
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.580
So where does the warmth come from?

6
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:20.933
A pea seed stores energy in its molecules.

7
00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:25.833
The fuel this lesson follows is glucose, a sugar that stores energy.

8
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:28.673
Plants make glucose by photosynthesis.

9
00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:31.733
Cells must break food down to use its energy.

10
00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:35.227
Cells do this by cellular respiration.

11
00:00:36.447 --> 00:00:41.027
Cellular respiration is how cells break down sugar with oxygen and release energy.

12
00:00:42.107 --> 00:00:43.347
Sugar and oxygen go in.

13
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:47.947
Carbon dioxide and water come out, along with the released energy.

14
00:00:48.907 --> 00:00:51.167
Oxygen is a gas in the air that cells use.

15
00:00:52.327 --> 00:00:54.647
Carbon dioxide is a gas that cells give off.

16
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.460
Most of this happens in small parts of the cell called mitochondria.

17
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.700
One is a mitochondrion.

18
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.320
A mitochondrion is an organelle where cells release energy from food.

19
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.480
Plant cells and animal cells both have them.

20
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.940
Almost all plant, animal and fungus cells have mitochondria.

21
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:17.563
The cell spends some of the released energy on work.

22
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:20.643
One kind of work is building new cell parts.

23
00:01:21.543 --> 00:01:24.003
Some energy always leaves as thermal energy.

24
00:01:24.943 --> 00:01:29.163
Thermal energy is the energy of moving particles, which you feel as warmth.

25
00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:32.103
The energy was already stored in the sugar.

26
00:01:33.143 --> 00:01:34.603
Respiration changes its form.

27
00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:37.543
Now the two thermoses make sense.

28
00:01:38.563 --> 00:01:40.883
Sprouting peas are alive, so their cells respire.

29
00:01:41.743 --> 00:01:46.023
Some released energy leaves as thermal energy, and the thermos holds it in.

30
00:01:46.923 --> 00:01:49.063
Boiled peas are dead, so they do not respire.

31
00:01:50.283 --> 00:01:52.063
Plants, fungi and animals all respire.

32
00:01:53.003 --> 00:01:55.023
Breathing is moving air in and out of your lungs.

33
00:01:55.883 --> 00:01:57.603
It is not the same as respiration.

34
00:01:58.583 --> 00:02:00.403
Respiration happens inside cells.

35
00:02:01.283 --> 00:02:04.603
Your cells use oxygen to release the energy stored in sugar.
